      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-251550",
      ":question/problem": "| $x_{i}$ | $y_{i}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $15$ | $13$ |\n| $15$ | $14$ |\n| $16$ | $15$ |\n| $17$ | $16$ |\n| $18$ | $17$ |\n\nConsider a sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}, x_{5}$ from a random variable $X$, and a sample $y_{1}, y_{2}, y_{3}, y_{4}, y_{5}$ from a random variable $Y$, shown in the table above. Given that the two samples are paired and $D = X - Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\u03bc$ and variance $\u03c3^{2} = (0.7)^{2}$, we wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $1%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that $\u03bc > 0$.\n\nThe table below shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$, where $Z\u223cN(0, 1)$.\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for the chosen test is $z \\approx$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe $1%$ **right-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\ge$\n{{field-2}}.\n\nAt the $1%$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-3}}\nevidence that $\u03bc > 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
